---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { jsonLd } from '../data/site';

const aboutPage = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'AboutPage',
  url: 'https://fetchsubs.com/about',
  name: 'About FetchSubs',
  mainEntity: {
    '@type': 'Person',
    name: 'Halit Burak Eroğlu',
    jobTitle: 'Software Developer',
    nationality: 'Türkiye',
    email: 'mailto:halitburakeroglu@gmail.com',
  },
};
---
<Layout
  title="About FetchSubs"
  fullTitle="About FetchSubs – Who Builds It and How It Works"
  description="FetchSubs is a free YouTube subtitle and transcript downloader built and run by Halit Burak Eroğlu, an independent developer in Türkiye."
>
<Fragment slot="head">
  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={jsonLd(aboutPage)} />
</Fragment>

<div class="doc-page">
    <h1>About FetchSubs</h1>

    <p>
        FetchSubs is a free tool for downloading the subtitles and transcripts of YouTube videos as SRT, VTT or plain text.
        It is an independent project, built and maintained by one person, and it is not affiliated with YouTube or Google.
    </p>

    <h2>Who runs FetchSubs</h2>
    <p>
        I'm <strong>Halit Burak Eroğlu</strong>, a software developer based in Türkiye. I started FetchSubs because I kept needing
        the text of lectures and talks I watched on YouTube: to search them, take notes, quote them accurately and paste them into
        other tools. YouTube shows a transcript panel, but there is no button to download it, and copying it by hand is slow and messy.
        FetchSubs is the tool I wanted for that job, made available to everyone.
    </p>
    <p>
        I write the code, run the server, answer the email and review every guide published on the blog. If something on the site is
        wrong or broken, it's my responsibility, and I want to hear about it.
    </p>

    <h2>How the tool works</h2>
    <p>
        When you paste a YouTube link, the FetchSubs server reads the list of caption tracks that YouTube already publishes for that
        video, both the creator's own subtitles and YouTube's automatic (speech-recognition) captions, using the open-source
        <a href="https://github.com/Tyrrrz/YoutubeExplode" target="_blank" rel="noopener">YoutubeExplode</a> library. When you pick a
        language and a format, the server downloads that one track and converts it to SRT, VTT or TXT on the fly.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Nothing is stored.</strong> Video links and subtitle files are processed in memory for your request and are not saved to a database.</li>
        <li><strong>No transcription.</strong> FetchSubs does not convert audio to text. If a video has no captions on YouTube, there is nothing to download.</li>
        <li><strong>No accounts, no installs.</strong> It works in any modern browser, on desktop and mobile.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        The tool is free and is supported by advertising. Ads never gate the download, and there are no paid tiers.
    </p>

    <h2>How the guides are written</h2>
    <p>
        The <a href="/blog">FetchSubs blog</a> explains practical tasks around subtitles and transcripts: getting a transcript on desktop or
        phone, choosing between <a href="/blog/srt-vtt-txt-subtitle-format-comparison-guide">SRT, VTT and TXT</a>, cleaning up auto-generated
        captions, or using a transcript with an AI assistant. Every guide is checked against the tool itself, and example output is taken from
        real downloads rather than made up. When a guide describes YouTube's own interface, it links to the official YouTube Help Center
        because that interface changes. Articles are updated when something changes, and the date of the last update is shown on the page.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>About AI assistance:</strong> some guides are drafted with the help of AI writing tools. Before publishing, I check every
        instruction, example and claim against the tool and against official documentation, and I remove anything I cannot verify.
        Earlier versions of some articles contained unsourced statistics and generic author names; those have been corrected.
    </p>

    <h2>Using subtitles responsibly</h2>
    <p>
        Subtitles belong to the people who created the videos. FetchSubs is meant for personal use, study, research, accessibility and
        working with your own content. Please respect creators' rights and YouTube's Terms of Service when you reuse a transcript. See the
        <a href="/terms">Terms of Use</a> for details, including how rights holders can contact me.
    </p>

    <h2>Get in touch</h2>
    <p>
        Bug reports, feature ideas and questions are all welcome. The <a href="/contact">contact page</a> explains what to include so I can help quickly.
    </p>

    <div class="contact-box" style="margin-top: 2rem; padding: 1.5rem; background: rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.1); border-radius: 1rem; border: 1px solid var(--accent-primary);">
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: var(--text-main); font-weight: 600;">Email</p>
        <a href="mailto:halitburakeroglu@gmail.com" style="color: var(--accent-primary); font-size: 1.2rem; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 700;">halitburakeroglu@gmail.com</a>
        <p style="margin-top: 1rem; font-size: 0.9rem;">I read every message myself.</p>
    </div>
</div>
</Layout>
